more importantly, the energy

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:33.470
that we see at
these conferences.

00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:36.310
It's really gratifying to see
it grow so much every year.

00:00:36.310 --> 00:00:38.636
So truly, thank you
for coming down today,

00:00:38.636 --> 00:00:40.510
and thank you for being
a part of that energy

00:00:40.510 --> 00:00:42.580
and a part of this conference.

00:00:42.580 --> 00:00:44.020
Particularly, thank
you for coming

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:47.020
at this, which is probably
the danger hour for a tech

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:50.710
conference-- right
after lunch on a Friday.

00:00:50.710 --> 00:00:54.510
So hopefully we'll be able
to keep you entertained.

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:56.710
What we're talking
about today is

00:00:56.710 --> 00:00:58.480
building high-performance
micro services

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:02.410
with Go, Kubernetes, and gRPC.

00:01:02.410 --> 00:01:04.989
And we actually
realized, when we

00:01:04.989 --> 00:01:08.620
were working through the
structure of this talk,

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:10.630
we kind of started
with a title, and we

00:01:10.630 --> 00:01:13.810
realized that you could
almost pick any single word

00:01:13.810 --> 00:01:16.240
in that sentence and
quite comfortably make

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:18.410
an hour-long talk out of it.

00:01:18.410 --> 00:01:20.992
And in fact, a number of
my colleagues are doing so.

00:01:20.992 --> 00:01:22.450
Even right after
the session, we've

00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:24.580
got Tim Hawken is
going to be diving

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:28.330
into the details of Kubernetes
networking and the latest

00:01:28.330 --> 00:01:29.220
there.

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:33.594
Dan Ciruli and Sep are going to
be talking about a thing called

00:01:33.594 --> 00:01:36.010
Cloud Endpoints-- which I'll
talk about a little bit, too.

00:01:36.010 --> 00:01:38.812
But there'll be
going deep on that.

00:01:38.812 --> 00:01:40.270
And then, across
the week we've had

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:41.920
a bunch of sessions
on these topics

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:44.140
that have usually picked
individual slices of this

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:45.970
and gone deep.

00:01:45.970 --> 00:01:47.890
So we were trying to
think of what we could

00:01:47.890 --> 00:01:49.600
do to make this interesting.

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:52.180
And we've realized
that what we could do

00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:55.209
is a complement to some of
these deep-dive sessions was

00:01:55.209 --> 00:01:57.250
something that is a little
broader and a bit more

00:01:57.250 --> 00:01:58.720
of an overview.

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:01.090
So what we want to
do today, is show how

00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:02.710
you can use these technologies.

00:02:02.710 --> 00:02:05.000
Hopefully, you've heard
of some, or all of them.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:06.730
But we want to show
how you can use them

00:02:06.730 --> 00:02:10.785
in a real world, practical,
grounded application.

00:02:10.785 --> 00:02:12.410
So that's what we're
going to do today.

00:02:12.410 --> 00:02:16.012
Today, we're going to build
and deploy an application

00:02:16.012 --> 00:02:17.470
to the Cloud Platform
that uses all

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:18.630
of these different services.

00:02:18.630 --> 00:02:20.296
And we're going to
show you a little bit

00:02:20.296 --> 00:02:21.970
about that process
and some techniques

00:02:21.970 --> 00:02:24.790
and some strategies you
can use to turn that

00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:25.879
into a robust workflow.

00:02:25.879 --> 00:02:27.670
We're going to talk a
little bit about some

00:02:27.670 --> 00:02:31.520
of these technologies as well
and how they help us get there.

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:34.970
So before we get
into the app itself,

00:02:34.970 --> 00:02:37.570
It's worth just recapping
a little bit of motivation.

00:02:37.570 --> 00:02:39.310
Hopefully,
microservices is a term

00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:41.800
that you've probably
heard before.

00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:44.710
It's not a new concept
for the most part.

00:02:44.710 --> 00:02:48.040
Fundamentally, it's
about taking apart

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:51.760
what might have previously been
a large monolithic codebase

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:54.670
and breaking it out into
discrete components that

00:02:54.670 --> 00:02:56.710
can be deployed independently.

00:02:56.710 --> 00:02:58.270
And particularly in the case--

00:02:58.270 --> 00:03:00.700
there's a number of
advantages for doing this.

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:02.680
But it's particularly
advantageous

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:04.750
when you are building
an application that

00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:06.910
has a large number
of developers on it.

00:03:06.910 --> 00:03:11.170
Being able to independently
deploy and manage and implement

00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:12.730
each of these
different components

00:03:12.730 --> 00:03:15.340
unlocks the development
things from each other

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:18.080
so that they can
work independently.

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:21.430
It can be an enormous
productivity boost.

00:03:21.430 --> 00:03:25.540
At Google, we're a very heavily
microservice-driven company,

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:27.040
or service-driven company.

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:28.880
When you interact with
a site like Gmail,

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:31.930
for example, when you go
and view mail.google.com,

00:03:31.930 --> 00:03:36.460
you are triggering
a cascade of RPCs

00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:38.440
across Google's data
centers in order

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:40.960
to retrieve the information
and serve you that page.

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:43.450
There's a whole ton of work
going on behind the scenes,

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:46.660
and as you do that, your RPC is
passing down through the code

00:03:46.660 --> 00:03:50.170
paths that dozens and, in many
cases, hundreds of engineers

00:03:50.170 --> 00:03:51.670
have worked on.

00:03:51.670 --> 00:03:55.510
So for us being able to deploy
these things independently

00:03:55.510 --> 00:03:57.810
is hugely powerful.

00:03:57.810 --> 00:03:59.839
So we've seen with
cloud, as well, there's

00:03:59.839 --> 00:04:02.380
been something of a resurgence,
and this is a design pattern.

00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:04.360
Again, for those who
were around in the '90s,

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:05.800
service-oriented
architecture was

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:08.410
another way of kind of
saying the same thing.

00:04:08.410 --> 00:04:10.360
The general design
pattern is not new,

00:04:10.360 --> 00:04:13.990
but a combination of elastic
workload provisioning.

00:04:13.990 --> 00:04:15.594
And some of these
technologies have

00:04:15.594 --> 00:04:18.010
helped accelerate it and push
it a little bit further back

00:04:18.010 --> 00:04:20.380
into the mainstream.

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:22.907
So it's becoming a
very popular design

00:04:22.907 --> 00:04:24.490
pattern, a very
useful one, especially

00:04:24.490 --> 00:04:25.990
in large organizations.

00:04:25.990 --> 00:04:30.340
But it comes with a couple
of important challenges.

00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:34.600
And perhaps the most immediate
and obvious challenges,

00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:38.270
when you're taking apart a
codebase, where previously, you

00:04:38.270 --> 00:04:41.400
were passing objects
around in memory,

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:44.930
now we're as often as not
passing them across the wire.

00:04:44.930 --> 00:04:48.370
So to send a message,
we have a bunch of work,

00:04:48.370 --> 00:04:52.629
to take a request, to
serialize it into a form

00:04:52.629 --> 00:04:54.670
that we can actually send
its bits down the wire.

00:04:54.670 --> 00:04:56.770
That request then needs
to get to a network card.

00:04:56.770 --> 00:04:58.570
That needs to get
translated into packets.

00:04:58.570 --> 00:05:00.653
Those packets need to get
sent, and again, there's

00:05:00.653 --> 00:05:02.910
many hours of discussion
you could have about that.

00:05:02.910 --> 00:05:04.260
All of this, then, needs
to happen in reverse

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:05.100
on the other end.

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:07.230
The message needs to be
deserialized, and then

00:05:07.230 --> 00:05:09.450
turned into something that an
application developer can use,

00:05:09.450 --> 00:05:11.616
some kind of in-memory
object that they can actually

00:05:11.616 --> 00:05:12.900
work with.

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:15.431
To do that can impose a
significant performance

00:05:15.431 --> 00:05:15.930
penalty.

00:05:15.930 --> 00:05:18.010
It's obviously a
lot more expensive

00:05:18.010 --> 00:05:21.900
from a compute-resource point
of view to do all of this work

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:23.250
every time you send a message.

00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:25.500
And particularly, when you
look at modern microservice

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:27.750
architectures, they
may be composed

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:34.350
of many tiers and many different
operations, like Fan-Out.

00:05:34.350 --> 00:05:36.990
As you push on this
design pattern,

00:05:36.990 --> 00:05:41.765
this cost of serialization
becomes more and more acute.

00:05:41.765 --> 00:05:42.470
Excuse me.

00:05:42.470 --> 00:05:44.340
So that's the first problem.

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:46.290
In concert with that
is another problem,

00:05:46.290 --> 00:05:48.980
which is just
network contention.

00:05:48.980 --> 00:05:53.180
Part of this is just simply
the amount of bytes and bits

00:05:53.180 --> 00:05:55.010
you have to send over
the wire when you're

00:05:55.010 --> 00:05:57.290
dealing with significant
volumes of scale,

00:05:57.290 --> 00:05:58.790
when you're dealing
with low latency

00:05:58.790 --> 00:06:01.220
or low, poor
reliability networks.

00:06:01.220 --> 00:06:04.220
Network contention
itself can be a problem.

00:06:04.220 --> 00:06:06.590
And if you're not careful,
not having a good model

00:06:06.590 --> 00:06:09.260
on your network architecture
can impede your ability

00:06:09.260 --> 00:06:13.149
to take up a design
pattern like this.

00:06:13.149 --> 00:06:15.440
And then, finally, as you're
breaking these things down

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:17.287
into multiple
components, usually,

00:06:17.287 --> 00:06:19.370
you're breaking them down
into multiple processes,

00:06:19.370 --> 00:06:21.390
multiple running programs.

00:06:21.390 --> 00:06:26.660
And when you take traditional
models of application

00:06:26.660 --> 00:06:28.880
isolation, application
containments,

00:06:28.880 --> 00:06:32.330
and apply that to
microservices, often this thing

00:06:32.330 --> 00:06:34.322
could cause a certain
degree of overhead

00:06:34.322 --> 00:06:35.780
that may be practical
for something

00:06:35.780 --> 00:06:38.324
that requires a whole machine
or several machines to run.

00:06:38.324 --> 00:06:40.490
But when you have a small
service that just takes up

00:06:40.490 --> 00:06:43.550
a couple of megabytes of
memory, maybe only needs

00:06:43.550 --> 00:06:47.870
a small amount of spindle usage,
some of the traditional models

00:06:47.870 --> 00:06:50.810
of managing the compute
resources that these services

00:06:50.810 --> 00:06:53.604
need aren't necessarily
as robust or as elastic

00:06:53.604 --> 00:06:54.770
as we would like them to be.

00:06:57.620 --> 00:07:00.260
And just to emphasize
that last point,

00:07:00.260 --> 00:07:02.340
it's worth mentioning
Bin Packing.

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:04.820
And so this is a somewhat
simplified view of the problem.

00:07:04.820 --> 00:07:07.970
But in a traditional model
of building applications,

00:07:07.970 --> 00:07:10.037
we may have separated
our component.

00:07:10.037 --> 00:07:11.870
We might have separated
our architecture out

00:07:11.870 --> 00:07:16.040
into a few services,
but we will typically

00:07:16.040 --> 00:07:18.380
have at least one machine
dedicated to each service,

00:07:18.380 --> 00:07:21.665
or maybe a cluster
dedicated to each service.

00:07:21.665 --> 00:07:24.040
And again, that's fine when
the services are quite large.

00:07:24.040 --> 00:07:27.550
It can often justify several
machines to run those.

00:07:27.550 --> 00:07:30.080
But when you have a large
number of small services,

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:31.460
this model becomes problematic.

00:07:31.460 --> 00:07:35.970
It's much more convenient
if we can run twofold.

00:07:35.970 --> 00:07:38.432
Firstly, run services
across multiple machines,

00:07:38.432 --> 00:07:39.890
and secondly, run
multiple services

00:07:39.890 --> 00:07:41.504
within a single machine.

00:07:41.504 --> 00:07:42.920
At Google, this
is a real problem.

00:07:42.920 --> 00:07:47.450
This is something we've started
encountering a long time ago

00:07:47.450 --> 00:07:51.500
because we had a large shared
bulk set of compute resources.

00:07:51.500 --> 00:07:54.290
We've got a large number of
developers deploying services

00:07:54.290 --> 00:07:55.310
into them.

00:07:55.310 --> 00:07:58.460
We wanted a way of being
able to efficiently manage

00:07:58.460 --> 00:08:01.196
how services were being
allocated to compute resource,

00:08:01.196 --> 00:08:02.570
without necessarily
having to get

00:08:02.570 --> 00:08:04.340
into the language of
individual machines

00:08:04.340 --> 00:08:06.530
to individual services.

00:08:06.530 --> 00:08:09.162
So this fine-grain Bin
Packing, if you can do it,

00:08:09.162 --> 00:08:11.120
turns out to be a real
advantage when deploying

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:12.200
these kinds of systems.

00:08:12.200 --> 00:08:15.290
Again, not strictly necessary,
not strictly required

00:08:15.290 --> 00:08:18.830
to couple this kind of
deployment to microservices,

00:08:18.830 --> 00:08:21.820
but it definitely
helps you, if you can.

00:08:21.820 --> 00:08:24.340
So, since we've been doing
so much of this at Google,

00:08:24.340 --> 00:08:28.010
and we've been doing
it for a while,

00:08:28.010 --> 00:08:30.880
we've spent a number of
engineering cycles building out

00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:33.520
technologies that can
help us in this process.

00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:35.758
We're going to talk about
three of them today.

00:08:35.758 --> 00:08:37.299
In many cases, what
you're looking at

00:08:37.299 --> 00:08:39.850
are the second, third, or
fourth generation attempts

00:08:39.850 --> 00:08:42.380
at solving these
kinds of problems.

00:08:42.380 --> 00:08:44.530
The first, and one
of the most exciting,

00:08:44.530 --> 00:08:47.710
is a thing that we open-sourced
reasonably recently

00:08:47.710 --> 00:08:48.220
called gRPC.

00:08:48.220 --> 00:08:51.010
It stands for Google
Remote Procedure Call.

00:08:51.010 --> 00:08:53.140
And gRPC is actually
a collection

00:08:53.140 --> 00:08:55.240
of libraries and
tools that allow

00:08:55.240 --> 00:09:01.780
you to create APIs, client
and servers, in a number

00:09:01.780 --> 00:09:03.010
of different languages.

00:09:03.010 --> 00:09:04.930
And it relies on
Protobufs, which

00:09:04.930 --> 00:09:08.410
are a strongly-typed and
very binary-efficient

00:09:08.410 --> 00:09:12.230
mechanism for serializing
and deserializing messages.

00:09:12.230 --> 00:09:16.000
And it's also built as
a transport on HTTP too.

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:17.680
So it's able to take
advantage of things

00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:19.150
like bi-directional streaming.

00:09:19.150 --> 00:09:21.430
And then, on top of
that, is robust library

00:09:21.430 --> 00:09:24.737
for things like flow control,
right management, retries,

00:09:24.737 --> 00:09:27.070
all of the work that ends up
being necessary in building

00:09:27.070 --> 00:09:28.480
a robust, powerful client.

00:09:28.480 --> 00:09:31.900
But it usually takes a few
attempts and a few mistakes,

00:09:31.900 --> 00:09:33.490
in order to get right.

00:09:33.490 --> 00:09:37.170
The other thing about it
is it's all open-source.

00:09:37.170 --> 00:09:39.600
Second thing that we're
going to use is Go.

00:09:39.600 --> 00:09:42.180
Go is the language
that absolutely

00:09:42.180 --> 00:09:45.030
has the cutest mascot--

00:09:45.030 --> 00:09:49.420
I'm sorry, smallest
runtime footprint.

00:09:49.420 --> 00:09:51.430
And this, again, turns
out to be important,

00:09:51.430 --> 00:09:53.096
especially when your
services are small,

00:09:53.096 --> 00:09:54.900
and you have a lot of them.

00:09:54.900 --> 00:09:56.430
It's not, again,
strictly necessary

00:09:56.430 --> 00:09:57.846
to have a small
runtime footprint,

00:09:57.846 --> 00:10:00.540
but when you can run your
process as efficiently

00:10:00.540 --> 00:10:02.370
as possible, with
as little memory

00:10:02.370 --> 00:10:04.930
and as little system
overhead as possible,

00:10:04.930 --> 00:10:07.950
this allows you to pack more
and more of that capacity

00:10:07.950 --> 00:10:12.277
into a finite amount of
compute, which saves you money.

00:10:12.277 --> 00:10:14.860
The other nice thing about Go,
when it comes to microservices,

00:10:14.860 --> 00:10:17.320
it has a ton of interesting
features in the language.

00:10:17.320 --> 00:10:18.730
But one of the things
it has that's important

00:10:18.730 --> 00:10:20.104
when it comes to
microservices is

00:10:20.104 --> 00:10:23.510
it has a modern and
efficient networking library.

00:10:23.510 --> 00:10:25.859
Go is a relatively new
language, which means, again,

00:10:25.859 --> 00:10:28.150
it's been able to learn some
of the lessons of the past

00:10:28.150 --> 00:10:29.740
in terms of just
how networking can

00:10:29.740 --> 00:10:31.859
be performed and abstracted.

00:10:31.859 --> 00:10:33.400
And it's been used
very, very heavily

00:10:33.400 --> 00:10:35.870
at Google for
networking applications.

00:10:35.870 --> 00:10:38.182
And so we've had a lot of
time and a lot of energy

00:10:38.182 --> 00:10:40.390
spent into making these
libraries as efficient and as

00:10:40.390 --> 00:10:42.010
optimal as possible.

00:10:42.010 --> 00:10:43.717
They're also nice and
easy to work with.

00:10:43.717 --> 00:10:46.300
And again, Go is open-source,
so there's a community out there

00:10:46.300 --> 00:10:47.099
that you can--

00:10:47.099 --> 00:10:49.390
you don't just get it for
free, but there's a community

00:10:49.390 --> 00:10:51.610
that you can engage with.

00:10:51.610 --> 00:10:55.070
And I don't want to make this
too academic a discussion,

00:10:55.070 --> 00:10:56.890
or too academic a
talk, but it is worth

00:10:56.890 --> 00:10:59.027
talking a little bit
about performance.

00:10:59.027 --> 00:11:01.110
The graph here is actually
from a public benchmark

00:11:01.110 --> 00:11:03.350
that the gRPC team published.

00:11:03.350 --> 00:11:06.210
They rerun this benchmark
with every release of gRPC.

00:11:06.210 --> 00:11:08.085
It's a thing called the
Ping Pong Test, where

00:11:08.085 --> 00:11:10.950
you have two VMs, and you just
take a message serialize it

00:11:10.950 --> 00:11:13.070
in a client, send
it to a server,

00:11:13.070 --> 00:11:15.820
deserialize it, serialize
it again, send it back,

00:11:15.820 --> 00:11:18.280
and repeat, I think
it's about 100 times.

00:11:18.280 --> 00:11:20.900
And measuring the
latency of that process.

00:11:20.900 --> 00:11:24.121
So it's a really good
measure of the performance

00:11:24.121 --> 00:11:26.620
of the language and the runtime
at dealing with this message

00:11:26.620 --> 00:11:27.430
passing.

00:11:27.430 --> 00:11:29.200
And you can see Go
does really well here.

00:11:29.200 --> 00:11:32.920
It's approaching the kind of
performance you get out of C++,

00:11:32.920 --> 00:11:36.355
and also, although it's
not on this list, Java.

00:11:36.355 --> 00:11:40.450
So, while gRPC is very much a
language-neutral technology,

00:11:40.450 --> 00:11:43.870
it works really well with
Go, which is why we like it.

00:11:43.870 --> 00:11:45.750
The last key piece in
this is Kubernetes.

00:11:45.750 --> 00:11:48.000
Again, there's a lot of talks
right now in Kubernetes,

00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:51.430
so, hopefully, you've
heard something about it.

00:11:51.430 --> 00:11:55.234
Kubernetes is the spiritual
successor, in many ways,

00:11:55.234 --> 00:11:57.400
to a technology that we've
been working on at Google

00:11:57.400 --> 00:11:59.140
for a long time, called Borg.

00:11:59.140 --> 00:12:00.940
Borg is our hosting
platform, that

00:12:00.940 --> 00:12:03.610
allows us to, again,
solve the Bin Packing

00:12:03.610 --> 00:12:06.100
problem-- taking a
large number of tasks,

00:12:06.100 --> 00:12:08.671
that large numbers of Googlers
is submitting to a system.

00:12:08.671 --> 00:12:10.420
And it effectively
acts as a giant solver,

00:12:10.420 --> 00:12:12.670
figuring out where is the
best place in my fleet

00:12:12.670 --> 00:12:14.480
to run that at any given time.

00:12:14.480 --> 00:12:15.400
And when these
workloads are not just

00:12:15.400 --> 00:12:16.660
being deployed all the
time, but they're also

00:12:16.660 --> 00:12:18.340
dynamic in terms
of the resources

00:12:18.340 --> 00:12:21.370
that they need to
have access to,

00:12:21.370 --> 00:12:23.890
being able to have a dynamic,
automatic system that's

00:12:23.890 --> 00:12:27.790
able to handle this
allocation is hugely powerful.

00:12:27.790 --> 00:12:30.700
With Kubernetes, we've taken
that exact same philosophy,

00:12:30.700 --> 00:12:32.980
and many of the same ideas,
and we've packaged app

00:12:32.980 --> 00:12:34.750
into an open-source project.

00:12:34.750 --> 00:12:36.970
Interestingly, it is
itself written in Go.

00:12:36.970 --> 00:12:41.530
Although, like gRPC,
it works perfectly well

00:12:41.530 --> 00:12:42.580
with any language.

00:12:42.580 --> 00:12:44.470
It's very much a
language-agnostic piece

00:12:44.470 --> 00:12:45.610
of infrastructure.

00:12:45.610 --> 00:12:49.460
Also, open-source and a very
thriving community of people

00:12:49.460 --> 00:12:50.710
building around that, as well.

00:12:50.710 --> 00:12:53.920
And I think, more contributions
of Kubernetes work commits

00:12:53.920 --> 00:12:56.170
have now come from outside
Google, rather than inside.

00:12:59.742 --> 00:13:02.200
So we want to build an app and
show how all of these things

00:13:02.200 --> 00:13:03.245
can fit together.

00:13:03.245 --> 00:13:06.170
If we can cut to
my laptop quickly--

00:13:06.170 --> 00:13:07.719
which I may need to wake up.

00:13:07.719 --> 00:13:09.010
So we're going to build an app.

00:13:09.010 --> 00:13:10.450
The app does this--

00:13:10.450 --> 00:13:13.492
it creates 3D animated
GIFs Super practical.

00:13:13.492 --> 00:13:14.950
I hope you can see
how this applies

00:13:14.950 --> 00:13:17.894
to your day-to-day
lives already.

00:13:17.894 --> 00:13:18.810
So it seems practical.

00:13:18.810 --> 00:13:20.268
One of these apps,
that is actually

00:13:20.268 --> 00:13:23.260
a little deceptive-- very simple
to use, very simple to show,

00:13:23.260 --> 00:13:26.170
but there's a lot going on,
in order to make this happen.

00:13:26.170 --> 00:13:31.720
I'll try this live and do my
ritual prep for the demo gods.

00:13:31.720 --> 00:13:34.030
Well, let's see
what this actually

00:13:34.030 --> 00:13:35.770
looks like to use this app.

00:13:35.770 --> 00:13:37.540
So a very simple form.

00:13:37.540 --> 00:13:39.430
I'm going to put in a name.

00:13:42.072 --> 00:13:43.280
I'm going to choose a mascot.

00:13:43.280 --> 00:13:44.238
You've seen the Go one.

00:13:44.238 --> 00:13:46.520
Again, cutest, right?

00:13:46.520 --> 00:13:49.740
I'll pick something
else, say gRPC.

00:13:49.740 --> 00:13:52.190
We go create an app.

00:13:52.190 --> 00:13:54.815
So we've now submitted a job.

00:13:54.815 --> 00:13:56.690
We're using a technique,
called Path Tracing,

00:13:56.690 --> 00:13:58.064
to actually generate
that 3D GIF,

00:13:58.064 --> 00:14:01.160
which is a particularly
computationally-intensive

00:14:01.160 --> 00:14:03.190
exercise.

00:14:03.190 --> 00:14:05.036
And so, in order to
make all of this happen,

00:14:05.036 --> 00:14:06.660
we've actually had
to do a ton of work.

00:14:06.660 --> 00:14:09.920
The first thing we had to do is
create a series of scene assets

00:14:09.920 --> 00:14:14.360
to render that image--
things, like materials files,

00:14:14.360 --> 00:14:16.340
objects files, a
bunch of metadata

00:14:16.340 --> 00:14:19.160
about light sources
and camera placement.

00:14:19.160 --> 00:14:20.880
And we actually generate
that dynamically

00:14:20.880 --> 00:14:22.880
for every single GIF that
needs to be requested.

00:14:22.880 --> 00:14:25.160
So we've gone and created
a bunch of those assets.

00:14:25.160 --> 00:14:28.460
The next thing we've
done is create a task

00:14:28.460 --> 00:14:30.770
to render each and
every frame of that GIF.

00:14:30.770 --> 00:14:32.266
There's about 10 of them.

00:14:32.266 --> 00:14:33.515
And so we then submitted that.

00:14:33.515 --> 00:14:34.710
And we're done.

00:14:34.710 --> 00:14:38.640
It's actually faster than it
took when I practiced this.

00:14:38.640 --> 00:14:42.121
So we had to submit a task for
every frame of this animation,

00:14:42.121 --> 00:14:44.120
and then we've then gone
and dispatched that out

00:14:44.120 --> 00:14:45.290
to our cluster.

00:14:45.290 --> 00:14:49.315
Our cluster has then gone and
run that rendering process.

00:14:49.315 --> 00:14:50.940
It's a pretty slow
library that we use.

00:14:50.940 --> 00:14:56.310
So it takes about 15 to 20
seconds to render each frame.

00:14:56.310 --> 00:14:58.229
So it's doing that
in the background.

00:14:58.229 --> 00:15:00.020
Pulls them all together
and synthesize them

00:15:00.020 --> 00:15:02.480
into an animated GIF.

00:15:02.480 --> 00:15:04.370
So quite a bit of
work there, just

00:15:04.370 --> 00:15:07.580
to handle all of that overhead.

00:15:07.580 --> 00:15:11.472
And if we switch
back to the slides,

00:15:11.472 --> 00:15:13.430
we can dive into the
architecture of this thing

00:15:13.430 --> 00:15:15.221
and take a look about
how we actually built

00:15:15.221 --> 00:15:17.381
this using these technologies.

00:15:17.381 --> 00:15:19.880
So what we're seeing here is,
basically, the set of services

00:15:19.880 --> 00:15:21.620
that we needed in order
to build this out.

00:15:21.620 --> 00:15:23.370
Would you believe five
services are needed

00:15:23.370 --> 00:15:26.720
to build 3D animated GIFs?

00:15:26.720 --> 00:15:28.772
So let's break this
down a little bit.

00:15:28.772 --> 00:15:30.230
Let's start with
the first service,

00:15:30.230 --> 00:15:32.810
the bottom-most service,
which is the Renderer.

00:15:32.810 --> 00:15:35.750
So the Renderer is actually a
very general-purpose service,

00:15:35.750 --> 00:15:38.480
which is nice if we ever
want to reuse animated GIF

00:15:38.480 --> 00:15:40.590
creation for any other purpose.

00:15:40.590 --> 00:15:44.697
What the Render service does
is take a request that includes

00:15:44.697 --> 00:15:47.030
references to all of those
assets that we talked about--

00:15:47.030 --> 00:15:51.020
so, again, objects, materials,
light sources, and so forth--

00:15:51.020 --> 00:15:54.810
will perform the task of
actually generating an image.

00:15:54.810 --> 00:15:57.200
It will store that image
in Google Cloud Storage,

00:15:57.200 --> 00:15:59.540
and then, it will
return in the response

00:15:59.540 --> 00:16:02.210
back to any upstream
caller path to where

00:16:02.210 --> 00:16:03.860
that single frame,
that single image,

00:16:03.860 --> 00:16:06.530
lives in Google Cloud Storage.

00:16:06.530 --> 00:16:09.140
On the other side of this, we
have the front-end service.

00:16:09.140 --> 00:16:11.082
The front-end service
is the service

00:16:11.082 --> 00:16:13.040
that we were actually
interacting with directly

00:16:13.040 --> 00:16:14.420
in the browser there.

00:16:14.420 --> 00:16:17.930
This is the,
essentially, HTTP server.

00:16:17.930 --> 00:16:20.889
So it's handling the form, it's
handling the form submission.

00:16:20.889 --> 00:16:22.430
If we were doing
user authentication,

00:16:22.430 --> 00:16:23.630
it would probably do that.

00:16:23.630 --> 00:16:25.088
And it does all
the kinds of things

00:16:25.088 --> 00:16:29.420
you would normally expect
to see in a web front-end.

00:16:29.420 --> 00:16:32.434
Handle it and serve static
assets, and things like that.

00:16:32.434 --> 00:16:34.600
There's not a lot of logic
in the front-end, though.

00:16:34.600 --> 00:16:37.220
We really wanted to keep the
logic out of the front-end,

00:16:37.220 --> 00:16:38.145
as much as possible.

00:16:38.145 --> 00:16:40.520
Because we never know if we
need to be building front-end

00:16:40.520 --> 00:16:41.240
ends for other things.

00:16:41.240 --> 00:16:43.260
Maybe there's a mobile
kiosk we want to build,

00:16:43.260 --> 00:16:45.890
or a mobile app that we want to
build that doesn't necessarily

00:16:45.890 --> 00:16:49.540
need to talk to a HTTP API.

00:16:49.540 --> 00:16:51.290
So we really wanted
to try and encapsulate

00:16:51.290 --> 00:16:54.410
as much of the business logic,
if you will, of the GIF creator

00:16:54.410 --> 00:16:55.970
into some other service.

00:16:55.970 --> 00:16:59.180
So we called this, of course,
the GIF Creator Service.

00:16:59.180 --> 00:17:03.185
GIF Creator does everything
in between our web front

00:17:03.185 --> 00:17:04.859
end and our Renderer.

00:17:04.859 --> 00:17:09.710
GIF Creator has
an API of its own.

00:17:09.710 --> 00:17:12.200
It takes an API request that
looks a lot like that form

00:17:12.200 --> 00:17:13.050
that you saw before.

00:17:13.050 --> 00:17:14.203
So there's a caption.

00:17:14.203 --> 00:17:15.119
You can pick a mascot.

00:17:15.119 --> 00:17:17.750
There's a couple of other
little bits of metadata.

00:17:17.750 --> 00:17:22.026
And when it receives that, it
will then do all of the work

00:17:22.026 --> 00:17:22.900
that we talked about.

00:17:22.900 --> 00:17:25.849
It will be creating
the scene assets.

00:17:25.849 --> 00:17:28.460
It will be storing them
in Google Cloud Storage.

00:17:28.460 --> 00:17:31.054
It will also be
responsible for calling out

00:17:31.054 --> 00:17:31.970
to the Render Service.

00:17:31.970 --> 00:17:36.020
So it will call a
Render's own API

00:17:36.020 --> 00:17:39.420
and perform the Fan-Out in
order to make that happen.

00:17:39.420 --> 00:17:41.840
We want to do that robustly,
so we've actually split this

00:17:41.840 --> 00:17:43.400
into a server and a worker.

00:17:43.400 --> 00:17:46.280
And we use Redis as our
State Management Service

00:17:46.280 --> 00:17:50.660
to track the status
of all of these jobs.

00:17:50.660 --> 00:17:52.305
GIF Creator handles all of that.

00:17:52.305 --> 00:17:56.030
It actually has its
RPC and its response.

00:17:56.030 --> 00:17:58.160
It doesn't send back the
GIF because we expect

00:17:58.160 --> 00:17:59.480
this process to take a while.

00:17:59.480 --> 00:18:01.640
Instead, we send
back a job status.

00:18:01.640 --> 00:18:05.120
Which the upstream front-end
can pull for, as necessary,

00:18:05.120 --> 00:18:09.130
in order to see where
this work is at.

00:18:09.130 --> 00:18:12.520
So GIF Creator is really the
workhorse of all of this.

00:18:12.520 --> 00:18:15.645
Of course, GIF Creator also does
that synthesis, the compositing

00:18:15.645 --> 00:18:17.770
of a set of frames, once
they've all been rendered,

00:18:17.770 --> 00:18:19.640
into a final image.

00:18:19.640 --> 00:18:23.300
So a lot of work going on here.

00:18:23.300 --> 00:18:25.330
Let's zoom in on the
APIs for a second.

00:18:25.330 --> 00:18:26.919
There's really two
APIs that we have

00:18:26.919 --> 00:18:28.210
for the two different services.

00:18:28.210 --> 00:18:31.300
The Render API and
the GIF Creator API.

00:18:31.300 --> 00:18:34.090
We've talked a little bit
about what those APIs do.

00:18:34.090 --> 00:18:36.790
But let's jump into
a little bit of code.

00:18:36.790 --> 00:18:39.010
We wanted to write
these APIs in gRPC.

00:18:39.010 --> 00:18:41.610
Again, we like the
fast serialization,

00:18:41.610 --> 00:18:42.792
the efficiency on the wire.

00:18:42.792 --> 00:18:44.500
We like all of the
retry and flow control

00:18:44.500 --> 00:18:47.070
that gRPC gives you.

00:18:47.070 --> 00:18:49.990
GRPC APIs typically
start with a document

00:18:49.990 --> 00:18:52.570
that looks a bit like the one
you've got on screen here.

00:18:52.570 --> 00:18:54.540
This is called a Protofile.

00:18:54.540 --> 00:18:57.430
A Protofile is actually
a language-agnostic

00:18:57.430 --> 00:19:01.270
domain-specific language
for describing an API.

00:19:01.270 --> 00:19:03.940
And it's describing two
things-- both the RPCs,

00:19:03.940 --> 00:19:05.329
which end points, you can call.

00:19:05.329 --> 00:19:07.120
Most crucially though,
it's also describing

00:19:07.120 --> 00:19:10.930
the messages that get passed in
the request and the response.

00:19:10.930 --> 00:19:12.910
And the takeaway,
really, from this screen

00:19:12.910 --> 00:19:20.080
is that this document
is quite strongly typed.

00:19:20.080 --> 00:19:22.450
You can see we've got
things like ENUMS here.

00:19:22.450 --> 00:19:25.534
We can describe quite
complex data structures.

00:19:25.534 --> 00:19:26.950
Although we haven't
had them here,

00:19:26.950 --> 00:19:29.670
we can have things like
repeating fields and arrays.

00:19:29.670 --> 00:19:33.462
And so it's a robust language
for describing message passing.

00:19:33.462 --> 00:19:35.170
And as I said, this
is language-agnostic.

00:19:35.170 --> 00:19:38.110
Although it looks a bit
like Go code, it isn't.

00:19:38.110 --> 00:19:41.090
But what it can be
used for is two things.

00:19:41.090 --> 00:19:44.470
One, is it's a fairly
human readable contract

00:19:44.470 --> 00:19:46.090
for describing what
the API should be.

00:19:46.090 --> 00:19:48.350
That in itself is
actually pretty useful.

00:19:48.350 --> 00:19:51.640
Two different teams who
need to talk via API

00:19:51.640 --> 00:19:53.950
can use this document to
effectively negotiate on

00:19:53.950 --> 00:19:58.250
what RPCs they need
from each other

00:19:58.250 --> 00:20:00.219
and what information they
need from each other.

00:20:00.219 --> 00:20:02.260
Especially when they work
in different languages,

00:20:02.260 --> 00:20:04.870
having a language-neutral
document like this

00:20:04.870 --> 00:20:08.007
can actually be a
really nice idea.

00:20:08.007 --> 00:20:10.090
The second thing that we
get out of this document,

00:20:10.090 --> 00:20:14.050
though, is an input for
a tool called Protoc.

00:20:14.050 --> 00:20:16.800
So Protoc is the
protocol buffer compiler.

00:20:16.800 --> 00:20:19.270
And what Protoc does
is take this document,

00:20:19.270 --> 00:20:22.370
or a document like it, and can
turn it into clients and server

00:20:22.370 --> 00:20:24.400
libraries in your
language of choice.

00:20:24.400 --> 00:20:28.737
I think, from memory, we support
about seven different languages

00:20:28.737 --> 00:20:29.320
at this point.

00:20:29.320 --> 00:20:31.095
And certainly Go is one of them.

00:20:31.095 --> 00:20:33.430
And I want to demo
it to you here.

00:20:33.430 --> 00:20:36.130
But when I run Protoc
over a file like this,

00:20:36.130 --> 00:20:38.380
I then get a library
generated, which

00:20:38.380 --> 00:20:40.360
I can then use in my code.

00:20:40.360 --> 00:20:42.790
So this is some actual code
from the GIF Creator Service.

00:20:42.790 --> 00:20:44.680
I've cut a bit out.

00:20:44.680 --> 00:20:46.930
But this gives you
an idea about how

00:20:46.930 --> 00:20:49.330
this generated code
from a Protofile

00:20:49.330 --> 00:20:50.494
can be used in my service.

00:20:50.494 --> 00:20:51.910
And if you've
worked with, really,

00:20:51.910 --> 00:20:53.410
any kind of client
server interface,

00:20:53.410 --> 00:20:55.222
this will look fairly familiar.

00:20:55.222 --> 00:20:56.680
There's a lot of
options and things

00:20:56.680 --> 00:20:59.595
that you can play around with
that we don't necessarily

00:20:59.595 --> 00:21:01.720
need to deal with by default.
But you can certainly

00:21:01.720 --> 00:21:04.780
get into things like
injecting middleware, things

00:21:04.780 --> 00:21:08.844
like supporting different
forms of authentication.

00:21:08.844 --> 00:21:11.260
But fundamentally, it's a very
simple, particularly in Go,

00:21:11.260 --> 00:21:12.970
construct to work with.

00:21:12.970 --> 00:21:15.490
And, crucially in
this, you can see

00:21:15.490 --> 00:21:17.860
that the objects that I'm
dealing with in my Go code

00:21:17.860 --> 00:21:18.970
are Go objects.

00:21:18.970 --> 00:21:20.080
I have Go structs.

00:21:20.080 --> 00:21:21.070
I have Go arrays.

00:21:21.070 --> 00:21:22.362
I have Go strings.

00:21:22.362 --> 00:21:24.820
So I'm working in a form that's
pretty idiomatic and pretty

00:21:24.820 --> 00:21:26.236
natural.

00:21:26.236 --> 00:21:28.360
I haven't had to deal with
any of the serialization

00:21:28.360 --> 00:21:29.692
and deserialization.

00:21:29.692 --> 00:21:30.900
GRPC has handled that for me.

00:21:33.630 --> 00:21:36.770
So now we'll get back
into the code in a second.

00:21:36.770 --> 00:21:41.180
But one of the things that we
found really trips people up--

00:21:41.180 --> 00:21:45.260
it tripped me up a lot when I
was first starting to work with

00:21:45.260 --> 00:21:46.670
these tools--

00:21:46.670 --> 00:21:48.500
is actually building
the pipeline

00:21:48.500 --> 00:21:51.560
that gets us from code,
like you saw before,

00:21:51.560 --> 00:21:54.057
to something that's actually
running up inside Kubernetes.

00:21:54.057 --> 00:21:55.640
It's complex because,
frankly, there's

00:21:55.640 --> 00:21:57.200
quite a few different
steps involved.

00:21:57.200 --> 00:22:00.020
And there's many different ways
of accomplishing those steps.

00:22:00.020 --> 00:22:02.000
There's not necessarily
one single blessed path

00:22:02.000 --> 00:22:03.826
that everybody uses.

00:22:03.826 --> 00:22:05.700
So we wanted to talk
about that a little bit,

00:22:05.700 --> 00:22:08.640
not to show the economical
way, necessarily,

00:22:08.640 --> 00:22:11.960
but a way, where we can
get from a code base

00:22:11.960 --> 00:22:14.134
to something that actually runs.

00:22:14.134 --> 00:22:15.550
As I said, there's
a lot of steps.

00:22:15.550 --> 00:22:19.440
The two high-level
steps, if you will,

00:22:19.440 --> 00:22:21.904
are the Build and
Packaging Phase--

00:22:21.904 --> 00:22:23.570
this is about taking
code and turning it

00:22:23.570 --> 00:22:26.905
into an image of binary assets.

00:22:26.905 --> 00:22:28.280
Typically, that's
a Docker image,

00:22:28.280 --> 00:22:32.579
but you can pick your
packaging format of choice.

00:22:32.579 --> 00:22:35.120
And you'll usually store that
in an asset registry somewhere.

00:22:35.120 --> 00:22:37.400
Maybe it's
Artifactory or DocHub.

00:22:37.400 --> 00:22:43.700
Or we're going to show Google
Container Registry, which

00:22:43.700 --> 00:22:49.100
comes with every
Google Cloud project.

00:22:49.100 --> 00:22:50.610
Second stage is
about deployment.

00:22:50.610 --> 00:22:52.259
I've built this
asset that describes

00:22:52.259 --> 00:22:53.300
everything I need to run.

00:22:53.300 --> 00:22:56.920
I then need to get it into
Kubernetes and deploy it.

00:22:56.920 --> 00:22:59.440
So let's double click
on that first stage.

00:22:59.440 --> 00:23:02.320
This is what the build
pipeline looks like--

00:23:02.320 --> 00:23:04.930
going from source
to an image for this

00:23:04.930 --> 00:23:06.580
app that we just showed you.

00:23:06.580 --> 00:23:10.210
And there's actually quite a
few different steps in there.

00:23:10.210 --> 00:23:14.500
And this is especially
true when you think about

00:23:14.500 --> 00:23:18.164
compiled languages,
like Go or C or Java.

00:23:18.164 --> 00:23:20.080
It's a lot more than
just getting some code up

00:23:20.080 --> 00:23:21.151
and running on a server.

00:23:21.151 --> 00:23:22.900
We really actually
rely on these pipelines

00:23:22.900 --> 00:23:26.300
in order to be able to do it.

00:23:26.300 --> 00:23:28.510
The first step is getting
the dependencies involved.

00:23:28.510 --> 00:23:29.796
Pretty straightforward.

00:23:29.796 --> 00:23:31.420
For those of you who
are Go developers,

00:23:31.420 --> 00:23:34.540
you'll know there's plenty of
dependency management tools.

00:23:34.540 --> 00:23:37.150
In this case, we used
Glide, although, I

00:23:37.150 --> 00:23:39.130
encourage folks to
check out Dep, which

00:23:39.130 --> 00:23:42.076
is gaining a lot of traction.

00:23:42.076 --> 00:23:44.200
But you use a package
management tool of some kind,

00:23:44.200 --> 00:23:45.700
to bring in all the
necessary dependencies

00:23:45.700 --> 00:23:47.491
that you have in your
application, anything

00:23:47.491 --> 00:23:48.820
you haven't checked in.

00:23:48.820 --> 00:23:51.160
Particularly important, if
this is on, say, a CI server,

00:23:51.160 --> 00:23:53.530
or it's a clean room
build of some kind.

00:23:53.530 --> 00:23:57.310
The next step is you need to
actually build your binaries.

00:23:57.310 --> 00:23:58.580
You need to run Go Build.

00:23:58.580 --> 00:24:02.410
Now, in our case, we have three
services that we're actually

00:24:02.410 --> 00:24:03.850
building ourselves.

00:24:03.850 --> 00:24:06.400
So we have three binaries,
one for each of them.

00:24:06.400 --> 00:24:08.500
In our case, all
of these binaries

00:24:08.500 --> 00:24:09.709
come from the same code base.

00:24:09.709 --> 00:24:11.375
And we're not just
going to package them

00:24:11.375 --> 00:24:12.249
into the same image.

00:24:12.249 --> 00:24:14.290
We're just going to invoke
that image differently

00:24:14.290 --> 00:24:15.400
for each service.

00:24:15.400 --> 00:24:17.200
Now, you don't have
to do it this way.

00:24:17.200 --> 00:24:19.960
Again, you could have separate
binaries, separate images,

00:24:19.960 --> 00:24:22.641
even separate languages for
every different service here.

00:24:22.641 --> 00:24:24.640
But for convenience, we've
packaged it into one.

00:24:24.640 --> 00:24:25.610
And we're at Google.

00:24:25.610 --> 00:24:28.274
We like them on a
repo thing I guess.

00:24:28.274 --> 00:24:29.440
But you have three binaries.

00:24:29.440 --> 00:24:30.856
And then, we have
these binaries--

00:24:30.856 --> 00:24:35.300
we need to package
them into an image.

00:24:35.300 --> 00:24:38.620
So in that image, this image is
this hermetically-sealed unit

00:24:38.620 --> 00:24:40.480
that contains everything
we need in order

00:24:40.480 --> 00:24:41.689
to be able to run our app.

00:24:41.689 --> 00:24:43.480
So obviously we need
the binaries in there.

00:24:43.480 --> 00:24:44.730
We don't need the source code.

00:24:44.730 --> 00:24:46.120
We don't need the build tools.

00:24:46.120 --> 00:24:48.010
But we do need a
few other assets.

00:24:48.010 --> 00:24:51.580
The web server, for example,
needs images, CSS files,

00:24:51.580 --> 00:24:55.480
templates, a bit of extra
stuff in order for it to work.

00:24:55.480 --> 00:24:58.810
The GIF Creator Service
needs canonical copies

00:24:58.810 --> 00:25:00.250
of all of its asset files.

00:25:00.250 --> 00:25:03.130
So there are a few resources
in there that are necessary,

00:25:03.130 --> 00:25:05.865
in addition to the binaries,
in order for this thing to run.

00:25:05.865 --> 00:25:07.240
And then, once
we've packaged it,

00:25:07.240 --> 00:25:10.470
we need to get this thing
into a registry, somehow.

00:25:10.470 --> 00:25:12.220
Now, when most people
start off with this,

00:25:12.220 --> 00:25:14.650
usually what they end up
doing is building usually

00:25:14.650 --> 00:25:16.840
some kind of, I don't
know, shell script file,

00:25:16.840 --> 00:25:20.350
or some other tool to help
coordinate all of these steps

00:25:20.350 --> 00:25:21.960
and bring them in together.

00:25:21.960 --> 00:25:23.140
And that works pretty well.

00:25:23.140 --> 00:25:25.900
Although, you do run into some
challenges with that model.

00:25:25.900 --> 00:25:27.550
One of them being,
you actually need

00:25:27.550 --> 00:25:29.110
copies of all of
these tools running,

00:25:29.110 --> 00:25:31.030
wherever you're running
that script in order

00:25:31.030 --> 00:25:33.430
to run the build process.

00:25:33.430 --> 00:25:35.887
So we found, at
Google, a lot of teams

00:25:35.887 --> 00:25:36.970
have this problem as well.

00:25:36.970 --> 00:25:39.160
A lot of our customers
have this problem.

00:25:39.160 --> 00:25:40.880
So we've come up with
a tool that helps.

00:25:40.880 --> 00:25:42.130
It's called Container Builder.

00:25:42.130 --> 00:25:44.940
We shipped it a couple
of days ago, actually.

00:25:44.940 --> 00:25:47.920
Container Builder does exactly
what it says on the tin.

00:25:47.920 --> 00:25:49.720
It takes source
code and packages it

00:25:49.720 --> 00:25:51.340
into a Docker image.

00:25:51.340 --> 00:25:53.530
And it supports a
language for describing

00:25:53.530 --> 00:25:55.820
these kinds of pipelines
that we've talked about.

00:25:55.820 --> 00:25:58.600
It's called, the YAML Syntax.

00:25:58.600 --> 00:26:00.430
The file is usually
called Cloudbuild.yaml.

00:26:00.430 --> 00:26:03.520
And again, I won't
dive into the details

00:26:03.520 --> 00:26:08.050
here, except to say that we
have a Cloud build YAML file.

00:26:08.050 --> 00:26:11.710
For this project, we've checked
it into the same repository

00:26:11.710 --> 00:26:13.240
that we have all
of our other code.

00:26:13.240 --> 00:26:16.690
And this describes the three
steps that are necessary

00:26:16.690 --> 00:26:17.950
in order to build our app.

00:26:17.950 --> 00:26:20.620
Everything is
included in this file.

00:26:20.620 --> 00:26:22.900
The way we describe steps
in Container Builder

00:26:22.900 --> 00:26:25.270
is actually with Docker
images themselves.

00:26:25.270 --> 00:26:26.920
So the nice thing
about this file

00:26:26.920 --> 00:26:29.080
is that it can actually
be taken and run

00:26:29.080 --> 00:26:32.780
by anyone else on my team, even
if they haven't necessarily

00:26:32.780 --> 00:26:38.270
installed all the tools like
Glide and Go and Docker before.

00:26:38.270 --> 00:26:40.980
So it's a pretty neat way of
describing a build process,

00:26:40.980 --> 00:26:42.400
and it's pretty general purpose.

00:26:42.400 --> 00:26:46.254
Again we chose things like Glide
and Go Build and Docker Build

00:26:46.254 --> 00:26:47.170
to package everything.

00:26:47.170 --> 00:26:49.480
But you can pick up other
tools if you wanted to.

00:26:49.480 --> 00:26:53.200
Practically, how do you actually
trigger a build like this?

00:26:53.200 --> 00:26:54.530
You've got two ways.

00:26:54.530 --> 00:26:57.460
One is, of course, there's
a GCloud command to do it.

00:26:57.460 --> 00:27:01.000
And that can be done from
any source tree that's

00:27:01.000 --> 00:27:02.200
sitting on your laptop.

00:27:02.200 --> 00:27:03.780
But we also have--

00:27:03.780 --> 00:27:06.130
and again, this was just
launched a couple of days ago--

00:27:06.130 --> 00:27:08.230
an extension to
contain a registry,

00:27:08.230 --> 00:27:09.370
called Build Triggers.

00:27:09.370 --> 00:27:13.120
Which allows you to watch
GitHub, any Bitbuckets,

00:27:13.120 --> 00:27:15.460
any Google source repository
that you might have set up.

00:27:15.460 --> 00:27:17.710
And every time you do a
commit to a particular branch,

00:27:17.710 --> 00:27:20.170
or a particular tag, or that
follows a particular pattern,

00:27:20.170 --> 00:27:21.628
you can automatically
trigger a new

00:27:21.628 --> 00:27:23.620
build in Container Registry.

00:27:23.620 --> 00:27:26.204
So I'll give you a
quick demo of that now.

00:27:26.204 --> 00:27:27.370
Hopefully, you can see this.

00:27:27.370 --> 00:27:29.328
I'm looking at Google
Source Repositories here.

00:27:29.328 --> 00:27:33.150
This is the source code of
all three of those services.

00:27:33.150 --> 00:27:36.213
Let's jump into front-end,
just to give you a sense of it.

00:27:36.213 --> 00:27:39.640
This is the front-end,
this is the Go code

00:27:39.640 --> 00:27:42.530
that produces the binary
for the front end.

00:27:42.530 --> 00:27:45.564
Some static assets in there.

00:27:45.564 --> 00:27:48.670
And some templates and a
few other little things.

00:27:48.670 --> 00:27:51.160
If you jump back out and
look at the root folder here,

00:27:51.160 --> 00:27:53.680
we're looking at
the master branch.

00:27:53.680 --> 00:27:57.289
We've also got some pretty
familiar-looking files that

00:27:57.289 --> 00:27:58.580
help us with the build process.

00:27:58.580 --> 00:28:00.640
So here's our Glide
file that describes

00:28:00.640 --> 00:28:02.342
all of our dependencies.

00:28:02.342 --> 00:28:04.300
Here's the Cloud Build
file, what we showed you

00:28:04.300 --> 00:28:05.300
a couple of seconds ago.

00:28:07.725 --> 00:28:09.470
Let's also go to
Docker file here, too.

00:28:09.470 --> 00:28:11.396
I mentioned there
was a packaging step.

00:28:11.396 --> 00:28:13.270
We're using Docker to
perform that packaging,

00:28:13.270 --> 00:28:16.630
and so it'll pull
from this Docker file.

00:28:16.630 --> 00:28:18.132
And you can see there--

00:28:18.132 --> 00:28:19.840
if you're not familiar
with Docker files,

00:28:19.840 --> 00:28:21.220
this probably won't
mean much to you.

00:28:21.220 --> 00:28:23.803
But if you are, the interesting
takeaway from this Docker file

00:28:23.803 --> 00:28:25.570
is there's really not
that much going on.

00:28:25.570 --> 00:28:27.700
All it's doing is
copying in the binaries

00:28:27.700 --> 00:28:30.687
that we built
upstream and adding

00:28:30.687 --> 00:28:32.270
the templates, and
a few other things.

00:28:32.270 --> 00:28:33.812
It's creating some
root certificates.

00:28:33.812 --> 00:28:35.769
But otherwise, it's really
not doing very much.

00:28:35.769 --> 00:28:37.103
It's a last mile packaging step.

00:28:37.103 --> 00:28:38.102
And this is really nice.

00:28:38.102 --> 00:28:39.670
It allows us to keep
all of our build

00:28:39.670 --> 00:28:41.182
tools out of our final image.

00:28:41.182 --> 00:28:43.390
And it allows us to create
something that really only

00:28:43.390 --> 00:28:46.920
has the bare minimum we
need in order to run it.

00:28:46.920 --> 00:28:49.417
So down here in Cloud Shell--

00:28:49.417 --> 00:28:50.750
hopefully, you can all see this.

00:28:53.300 --> 00:28:54.540
So this is a local VM.

00:28:54.540 --> 00:28:57.820
I've checked out, and you
can see that same repository.

00:28:57.820 --> 00:29:00.240
So I'm going to try and
make a quick change here.

00:29:00.240 --> 00:29:02.771
And for the sake
of this demo, I'm

00:29:02.771 --> 00:29:04.020
going to make a boring change.

00:29:04.020 --> 00:29:05.970
I'm just going to jump
into the front-end.

00:29:11.330 --> 00:29:17.900
And what I'm going to do is, I'm
going to make the form that you

00:29:17.900 --> 00:29:19.334
fill out to create
a GIF, I'm just

00:29:19.334 --> 00:29:20.750
going to change
the color of that,

00:29:20.750 --> 00:29:24.230
just to prove this
deployment flow to us.

00:29:24.230 --> 00:29:27.150
So if look at that,
it's form.html.

00:29:35.818 --> 00:29:36.320
OK.

00:29:36.320 --> 00:29:37.477
We have a header here.

00:29:37.477 --> 00:29:39.310
So I'm going to do
something really trivial.

00:29:39.310 --> 00:29:47.790
I'm just going to add
something like that.

00:29:47.790 --> 00:29:49.400
So I made the change-- simple.

00:29:49.400 --> 00:29:51.108
Let's commit that
back to our repo.

00:30:11.931 --> 00:30:12.430
OK.

00:30:12.430 --> 00:30:15.460
So we've pushed that.

00:30:15.460 --> 00:30:19.490
So now, if we jump over
to Container Registry--

00:30:19.490 --> 00:30:20.764
let me give you some room.

00:30:20.764 --> 00:30:22.930
If we jump over to Container
Registry, you see here,

00:30:22.930 --> 00:30:24.796
we have this list
of Build Triggers.

00:30:24.796 --> 00:30:26.170
So we've set up
a trigger already

00:30:26.170 --> 00:30:28.600
to watch that repository,
and every time we push

00:30:28.600 --> 00:30:31.420
to the master branch, it's
going to automatically trigger

00:30:31.420 --> 00:30:32.370
a Build for us.

00:30:32.370 --> 00:30:34.120
If we going jump in
to Build History here,

00:30:34.120 --> 00:30:37.250
we can see that build
is actually kicking off.

00:30:37.250 --> 00:30:40.104
And I won't bore you with
too much of the detail here,

00:30:40.104 --> 00:30:42.020
except to say, here's
the logs of the process.

00:30:42.020 --> 00:30:44.550
Right now, it's
running Glide to bring

00:30:44.550 --> 00:30:46.050
in all of the
dependencies, and then

00:30:46.050 --> 00:30:48.974
it'll continue on with the
build and the remaining steps.

00:30:48.974 --> 00:30:50.390
Something interesting
to note here

00:30:50.390 --> 00:30:52.470
is every build is
given a Build ID.

00:30:52.470 --> 00:30:54.650
This is just a UUID.

00:30:54.650 --> 00:30:58.010
And in our Cloud Build file--

00:30:58.010 --> 00:31:00.700
actually, let me show
you that really quick.

00:31:00.700 --> 00:31:03.050
In our Cloud Build
file, we've actually

00:31:03.050 --> 00:31:10.890
configured it to generate our
Docker image with a tag based

00:31:10.890 --> 00:31:11.750
on that Build ID.

00:31:16.140 --> 00:31:18.900
So we've asked to insert the
Build ID into the final image.

00:31:22.439 --> 00:31:23.230
So let's jump back.

00:31:23.230 --> 00:31:25.110
That might be done by now.

00:31:25.110 --> 00:31:27.330
And it is.

00:31:27.330 --> 00:31:28.740
So there's our build.

00:31:28.740 --> 00:31:31.611
If we jump into
Container Registry--

00:31:31.611 --> 00:31:33.231
and look.

00:31:33.231 --> 00:31:34.230
And we have a new image.

00:31:34.230 --> 00:31:36.231
Great.

00:31:36.231 --> 00:31:38.230
And just a quick look at
the size of that image.

00:31:38.230 --> 00:31:39.210
It's only 15 megabytes.

00:31:39.210 --> 00:31:41.170
And if you're used to dealing
with your Docker images built

00:31:41.170 --> 00:31:43.149
from Ubuntu that have a
ton of tooling in them,

00:31:43.149 --> 00:31:44.440
15 megabytes is nice and small.

00:31:47.220 --> 00:31:49.080
So now, we have an image.

00:31:49.080 --> 00:31:52.630
Next, we want to get
it into production.

00:31:52.630 --> 00:31:54.570
So now, we want to
talk about deployment.

00:31:54.570 --> 00:31:55.990
Before we do that, though--

00:31:55.990 --> 00:31:57.448
so you'll notice,
again, this thing

00:31:57.448 --> 00:31:59.280
has a tag based on its Build ID.

00:31:59.280 --> 00:32:01.530
For Kubernetes, this
can be really useful.

00:32:01.530 --> 00:32:04.710
Because we're going to use
these tags in our deployment

00:32:04.710 --> 00:32:05.585
manifests.

00:32:05.585 --> 00:32:07.710
Now, we could use the UUID
that I got created here,

00:32:07.710 --> 00:32:09.720
but that's a lot to
remember and a lot to type.

00:32:09.720 --> 00:32:13.350
So I'm actually going to
add a second tag here called

00:32:13.350 --> 00:32:23.130
Demo Fix, which we can
use a little later when

00:32:23.130 --> 00:32:24.930
we're doing a deployment.

00:32:24.930 --> 00:32:26.950
So if we can jump
back to the slides.

00:32:26.950 --> 00:32:29.575
Let's just talk about what this
deployment actually looks like.

00:32:31.590 --> 00:32:35.710
So what does this thing actually
look like inside Kubernetes?

00:32:35.710 --> 00:32:39.310
Kubernetes has a number
of constructs that are

00:32:39.310 --> 00:32:41.710
really useful in this process.

00:32:41.710 --> 00:32:44.950
And again, it could be
easily an hour talk,

00:32:44.950 --> 00:32:47.770
just describing the different
moving parts of Kubernetes

00:32:47.770 --> 00:32:50.827
that come into play when
you're running a real service.

00:32:50.827 --> 00:32:52.910
We're really only going
to talk about two of them.

00:32:52.910 --> 00:32:55.270
The first is deployments.

00:32:55.270 --> 00:33:02.620
So a deployment is a
concatenated object

00:33:02.620 --> 00:33:05.020
in Kubernetes that
actually, in turn,

00:33:05.020 --> 00:33:07.990
describes a number of other
underlying constructs--

00:33:07.990 --> 00:33:12.280
a replica set and pods
and pod configurations.

00:33:12.280 --> 00:33:15.610
And it provides a really
nice declarative way

00:33:15.610 --> 00:33:19.302
of describing how all of
these things are constructed.

00:33:19.302 --> 00:33:21.010
Fundamentally, what
a deployment is doing

00:33:21.010 --> 00:33:23.440
is telling Kubernetes how
to run this image, how

00:33:23.440 --> 00:33:25.170
to run this code.

00:33:25.170 --> 00:33:29.680
And we actually have
an example of what

00:33:29.680 --> 00:33:30.790
a deployment looks like.

00:33:30.790 --> 00:33:35.470
This is a slightly
simplified deployment spec.

00:33:35.470 --> 00:33:37.450
What this deployment
is describing is,

00:33:37.450 --> 00:33:39.430
take an image that
we have sitting

00:33:39.430 --> 00:33:41.830
in a registry somewhere.

00:33:41.830 --> 00:33:43.367
Run that image
using this command.

00:33:43.367 --> 00:33:44.950
So this is the
front-end service here.

00:33:44.950 --> 00:33:47.620
So we want to call
that Front-End Binary,

00:33:47.620 --> 00:33:48.905
that we built before.

00:33:48.905 --> 00:33:51.030
Describe any of the metadata
that this thing needs.

00:33:51.030 --> 00:33:52.154
So what ports does it need?

00:33:52.154 --> 00:33:54.112
What environment
variables does it need?

00:33:54.112 --> 00:33:55.570
You can describe
other constraints,

00:33:55.570 --> 00:33:57.910
like what's the minimum amount
of CPU this thing needs?

00:33:57.910 --> 00:33:59.770
How much memory does it need?

00:33:59.770 --> 00:34:02.350
But fundamentally,
pretty simple.

00:34:02.350 --> 00:34:04.589
And how many
replicas do we need?

00:34:04.589 --> 00:34:06.130
How many copies of
this process do we

00:34:06.130 --> 00:34:09.690
want running across our cluster.

00:34:09.690 --> 00:34:12.719
The other component is services.

00:34:12.719 --> 00:34:14.550
So it's going to
get a little meta,

00:34:14.550 --> 00:34:17.280
but we're going to build
services for services.

00:34:17.280 --> 00:34:22.350
So a Kubernetes service
is a way of providing

00:34:22.350 --> 00:34:28.000
a simple static endpoint
that we can use when we talk,

00:34:28.000 --> 00:34:29.830
used to help us
in talking to all

00:34:29.830 --> 00:34:31.080
of these different components.

00:34:31.080 --> 00:34:34.050
With deployments, we
might be describing

00:34:34.050 --> 00:34:37.351
a number of different processes
running all over our cluster.

00:34:37.351 --> 00:34:39.600
We need a way of being able
to discover where they are

00:34:39.600 --> 00:34:41.670
and being able to
get traffic to them.

00:34:41.670 --> 00:34:44.340
So a service in Kubernetes
helps us do that.

00:34:44.340 --> 00:34:45.840
The way you can use
a service really

00:34:45.840 --> 00:34:47.159
depends on your deployment.

00:34:47.159 --> 00:34:50.520
It's a very flexible descriptor.

00:34:50.520 --> 00:34:54.120
In our case, though, the three
services we're building all

00:34:54.120 --> 00:34:55.560
work basically the same way.

00:34:55.560 --> 00:34:58.800
We want to have a
stable, internal DNS

00:34:58.800 --> 00:35:00.192
name for each of them.

00:35:00.192 --> 00:35:01.650
so that if we need
to refer to them

00:35:01.650 --> 00:35:03.275
from another service
inside Kubernetes,

00:35:03.275 --> 00:35:06.442
we can just look it
up by its DNS name.

00:35:06.442 --> 00:35:08.400
And then, what we want
is a load balancer which

00:35:08.400 --> 00:35:12.550
can dynamically route traffic
to any of the pods, i.e.

00:35:12.550 --> 00:35:16.000
the running versions of this
process, at any one time.

00:35:16.000 --> 00:35:18.540
So this manifest file
describes everything we need,

00:35:18.540 --> 00:35:21.247
in order to be able to do that.

00:35:21.247 --> 00:35:23.330
And we have one of these
for each of our services.

00:35:23.330 --> 00:35:24.870
We also have one for
the Render Service

00:35:24.870 --> 00:35:26.744
that we're running inside
Kubernetes as well.

00:35:29.694 --> 00:35:31.610
So now, we're going to
run another quick demo.

00:35:36.340 --> 00:35:38.790
So if we can switch-- great.

00:35:38.790 --> 00:35:40.830
So we've built this image.

00:35:40.830 --> 00:35:44.250
Let's get that image
into Kubernetes.

00:35:44.250 --> 00:35:51.080
So in our Source Tree, we
have this directory k8s.

00:35:51.080 --> 00:35:54.407
And in that k8s directory,
we have a set of YAML files

00:35:54.407 --> 00:35:55.740
that describe these deployments.

00:35:55.740 --> 00:35:58.650
Basically, the same thing
you just saw on screen.

00:35:58.650 --> 00:36:01.710
Now, we have three services.

00:36:01.710 --> 00:36:03.930
And each of those has
a deployment manifest

00:36:03.930 --> 00:36:06.480
and a service manifest.

00:36:06.480 --> 00:36:09.750
So in our case, what we want
to do is update the front-end.

00:36:09.750 --> 00:36:12.540
And really, all we want to do
is tell that front-end service,

00:36:12.540 --> 00:36:14.310
instead of pointing
to the old image,

00:36:14.310 --> 00:36:19.490
now we want you to be
running the new image.

00:36:19.490 --> 00:36:29.560
So if I increase this a little
bit and jump into that file,

00:36:29.560 --> 00:36:31.300
you can see here is the file.

00:36:31.300 --> 00:36:32.910
I like environment variable.

00:36:32.910 --> 00:36:34.951
So I've created a bunch
of them for this project.

00:36:38.360 --> 00:36:41.940
And this here is
the image that's

00:36:41.940 --> 00:36:43.410
being used to render
the front-end.

00:36:43.410 --> 00:36:45.535
You can incidentally run
multiple, different images

00:36:45.535 --> 00:36:49.260
inside a single what we call
pod in Kubernetes parlance.

00:36:49.260 --> 00:36:52.817
But in our case, we're
just going to do one.

00:36:52.817 --> 00:36:54.650
And we're going to pull
from a different tag

00:36:54.650 --> 00:36:56.580
than the one we did previously.

00:36:56.580 --> 00:36:59.270
And I think-- what was it again?

00:36:59.270 --> 00:37:00.240
Was it Demo Fix?

00:37:03.180 --> 00:37:04.160
Yeah, Demo Fix.

00:37:11.510 --> 00:37:13.470
OK.

00:37:13.470 --> 00:37:14.920
So we've updated that file.

00:37:14.920 --> 00:37:19.857
Now we need to tell
Kubernetes to deploy it.

00:37:19.857 --> 00:37:20.690
So let's go do that.

00:37:33.220 --> 00:37:34.440
OK.

00:37:34.440 --> 00:37:35.610
Deployment replaced.

00:37:35.610 --> 00:37:39.210
Now, it's worth unpacking that
last section a little bit,

00:37:39.210 --> 00:37:41.850
because a lot just happened.

00:37:41.850 --> 00:37:45.360
We didn't tell Kubernetes that
we wanted to replace an image.

00:37:45.360 --> 00:37:47.760
What we did was tell
Kubernetes that we

00:37:47.760 --> 00:37:50.520
would like the state of
the system to change,

00:37:50.520 --> 00:37:52.240
to match this new file.

00:37:52.240 --> 00:37:55.830
And one of the things that's
different in that new file is

00:37:55.830 --> 00:37:57.252
the image has changed.

00:37:57.252 --> 00:37:59.460
It's kind of important that
we described it that way.

00:37:59.460 --> 00:38:00.668
We didn't say, make a change.

00:38:00.668 --> 00:38:02.790
We just said, we have a
new idea of what we want

00:38:02.790 --> 00:38:04.560
the state of our system to be.

00:38:04.560 --> 00:38:08.274
Kubernetes, go and figure
out how to make that happen.

00:38:08.274 --> 00:38:09.690
Now we can provide
hints as to how

00:38:09.690 --> 00:38:12.316
Kubernetes can make that happen,
like deployment policies.

00:38:12.316 --> 00:38:14.940
But, at the end of the day, this
is Kubernetes's responsibility

00:38:14.940 --> 00:38:17.100
to figure out how
to make it work.

00:38:17.100 --> 00:38:20.100
And this is, again, a really
powerful idea in Kubernetes,

00:38:20.100 --> 00:38:22.050
that you can
declaratively describe

00:38:22.050 --> 00:38:24.390
the state of your system and
have Kubernetes figure out

00:38:24.390 --> 00:38:28.730
all the phony details about
how it should actually work.

00:38:28.730 --> 00:38:31.070
So let's actually-- a
moment of truth here--

00:38:31.070 --> 00:38:33.060
see if this actually worked.

00:38:33.060 --> 00:38:36.800
So I'm going to jump into
our Kubernetes Dashboard.

00:38:36.800 --> 00:38:38.664
You can see, again,
all of the deployments.

00:38:38.664 --> 00:38:39.830
This is our running cluster.

00:38:39.830 --> 00:38:41.413
This is running
across 10 nodes, which

00:38:41.413 --> 00:38:44.670
might be a little excessive
for a GIF Creator.

00:38:44.670 --> 00:38:48.830
We have a front-end
deployment here.

00:38:48.830 --> 00:38:50.920
And we can see, a
minute ago, this thing

00:38:50.920 --> 00:38:53.460
received a new replica set.

00:38:53.460 --> 00:38:57.880
So what Kubernetes
did, in the background,

00:38:57.880 --> 00:39:00.880
was create a new
replica set that

00:39:00.880 --> 00:39:03.610
describes the set of
running pods that we need.

00:39:03.610 --> 00:39:06.351
This new replica set would
have the new pod configuration

00:39:06.351 --> 00:39:06.850
in it.

00:39:06.850 --> 00:39:08.320
I with the new image.

00:39:08.320 --> 00:39:14.080
And it will gradually increase
the size of that replica set,

00:39:14.080 --> 00:39:16.390
while spinning down the old one.

00:39:16.390 --> 00:39:19.720
And so what you get is
a graceful deployment

00:39:19.720 --> 00:39:22.300
as we shift from one
mode of the system

00:39:22.300 --> 00:39:23.737
to another mode of the system.

00:39:23.737 --> 00:39:24.820
So that actually happened.

00:39:24.820 --> 00:39:26.140
In our case, it's a
pretty simple system,

00:39:26.140 --> 00:39:28.723
and we're not really doing much
in the way of health checking,

00:39:28.723 --> 00:39:31.420
or any other thing that can
potentially slow this down.

00:39:31.420 --> 00:39:33.700
So in our case, it just
happened in a second or so.

00:39:33.700 --> 00:39:36.040
But we have now a
new replica set.

00:39:36.040 --> 00:39:38.320
And you can see here,
it has two pods in them.

00:39:38.320 --> 00:39:40.420
Pods aren't doing very much yet.

00:39:40.420 --> 00:39:43.650
But if we jump into
one of them, we

00:39:43.650 --> 00:39:47.150
can see that this is now
running with a new image.

00:39:47.150 --> 00:39:49.590
And it looks like it
started perfectly well.

00:39:49.590 --> 00:39:52.940
So now, if we go back
to our GIF Creator,

00:39:52.940 --> 00:39:54.976
we go back to that entry form.

00:39:54.976 --> 00:39:56.250
Our text is in red.

00:39:56.250 --> 00:39:58.510
So our change went through.

00:39:58.510 --> 00:40:03.502
[APPLAUSE]

00:40:03.502 --> 00:40:05.210
That is, hands down,
the biggest applause

00:40:05.210 --> 00:40:06.575
I've ever got for
changing a bit of CSS.

00:40:06.575 --> 00:40:07.491
So thank you for that.

00:40:09.950 --> 00:40:14.367
So now, we have a running
app, running in a cluster.

00:40:14.367 --> 00:40:15.950
We only got a little
bit of time left.

00:40:15.950 --> 00:40:17.570
And I want some for
Q&amp;A. So I'm going

00:40:17.570 --> 00:40:20.210
to do a whirlwind tour
of some of the things

00:40:20.210 --> 00:40:23.360
that you get when you run this
stuff inside Google Cloud.

00:40:23.360 --> 00:40:25.777
Everything I've shown you up
until now, for the most part,

00:40:25.777 --> 00:40:27.568
with the exception of
Google Cloud Storage,

00:40:27.568 --> 00:40:29.460
perhaps, you can run
basically anywhere.

00:40:29.460 --> 00:40:32.330
In fact, most of it, you can run
on your laptop, if you want to.

00:40:32.330 --> 00:40:34.599
And again, all of this
stuff is open-source.

00:40:34.599 --> 00:40:35.390
That's pretty nice.

00:40:35.390 --> 00:40:36.830
It means, you don't really
need to pick Google,

00:40:36.830 --> 00:40:38.480
in order to get started
with this stuff.

00:40:38.480 --> 00:40:40.670
But if you do happen to be
running it on the platform,

00:40:40.670 --> 00:40:42.461
you get a couple of
interesting advantages.

00:40:42.461 --> 00:40:45.500
The first, of course, is you get
Google Container Engine, which

00:40:45.500 --> 00:40:47.960
takes away a lot of the
headaches of actually running

00:40:47.960 --> 00:40:48.950
a Kubernetes cluster.

00:40:48.950 --> 00:40:50.540
And it can do a lot
of things for you,

00:40:50.540 --> 00:40:52.850
including automatically
patching nodes,

00:40:52.850 --> 00:40:56.730
automatically standing up,
say, Google Cloud Platform

00:40:56.730 --> 00:41:00.650
Load balances in order
to make your service run.

00:41:00.650 --> 00:41:04.570
It can do dynamic auto-scaling.

00:41:04.570 --> 00:41:06.890
A bunch of other nice
plug-in features there.

00:41:06.890 --> 00:41:09.090
And it takes a couple
of clicks to use.

00:41:09.090 --> 00:41:09.980
But there's a couple
of other features

00:41:09.980 --> 00:41:11.979
that turn out to be pretty
powerful on the Cloud

00:41:11.979 --> 00:41:15.305
Platform that are worth
touring through briefly.

00:41:15.305 --> 00:41:16.430
We saw a Container Builder.

00:41:16.430 --> 00:41:17.820
We saw Source Repositories.

00:41:17.820 --> 00:41:20.360
We saw the Container Registry.

00:41:20.360 --> 00:41:25.570
We also have a built-in tracing
system, which works really well

00:41:25.570 --> 00:41:27.670
with Kubernetes and gRPC.

00:41:27.670 --> 00:41:32.770
So I won't bore you with
a full tour of this,

00:41:32.770 --> 00:41:34.630
but this allows us
to do distributed

00:41:34.630 --> 00:41:36.130
tracing across a cluster.

00:41:36.130 --> 00:41:40.480
Distributor tracing is
actually really, really useful

00:41:40.480 --> 00:41:41.890
in situations like this.

00:41:41.890 --> 00:41:43.510
One of the first
things, that I wanted

00:41:43.510 --> 00:41:45.430
to do once I deployed
this app, was

00:41:45.430 --> 00:41:47.020
figure out why it
took so long for us

00:41:47.020 --> 00:41:48.772
to render a bunch of frames.

00:41:48.772 --> 00:41:50.230
So by jumping into
tracing, you are

00:41:50.230 --> 00:41:54.470
able to understand, even
as a job progresses,

00:41:54.470 --> 00:41:55.720
where the time is being taken.

00:41:55.720 --> 00:41:57.136
Is it being taken
up in rendering,

00:41:57.136 --> 00:41:58.420
or passing this Cloud Storage?

00:41:58.420 --> 00:42:00.670
Or is it in a Go binary?

00:42:00.670 --> 00:42:03.220
Distributed tracing allows
you to instrument your code.

00:42:03.220 --> 00:42:06.310
And when it's distributed,
the nice artifact of this

00:42:06.310 --> 00:42:11.380
is you can parse trace context
across multiple running

00:42:11.380 --> 00:42:11.900
processes.

00:42:11.900 --> 00:42:14.780
GRPC, in particular, makes
it really easy to do that.

00:42:14.780 --> 00:42:18.070
But by doing so, you can
actually ditrace a request,

00:42:18.070 --> 00:42:21.490
all the way from when the user
requested it on our front-end,

00:42:21.490 --> 00:42:24.280
down through to the fanned-out
tasks that were being run

00:42:24.280 --> 00:42:25.150
in the Render--

00:42:25.150 --> 00:42:28.720
and all the way back
up to the final job.

00:42:28.720 --> 00:42:32.174
I won't demo that now, but would
make for a good follow up talk.

00:42:32.174 --> 00:42:34.090
Incidentally, what you're
looking at right now

00:42:34.090 --> 00:42:36.860
is the breakdown of
latency over time.

00:42:36.860 --> 00:42:39.610
And you can see there's a few
experimental tasks there, which

00:42:39.610 --> 00:42:42.557
took a really long time to run.

00:42:42.557 --> 00:42:44.140
And generally, you've
got a clustering

00:42:44.140 --> 00:42:46.098
that looks a little bit
like what you'd expect.

00:42:46.098 --> 00:42:48.550
Some of these dots represent
our front-end service,

00:42:48.550 --> 00:42:50.950
which is really fast.

00:42:50.950 --> 00:42:52.540
And responds to
requests very quickly,

00:42:52.540 --> 00:42:53.620
terminates very quickly.

00:42:53.620 --> 00:42:55.820
Some of these represent
spans of longer jobs--

00:42:55.820 --> 00:42:58.612
e.g., the entire process of
rendering an image end to end.

00:42:58.612 --> 00:43:00.070
Or just calling
the Render Service,

00:43:00.070 --> 00:43:03.760
which takes a bit
more time, too.

00:43:03.760 --> 00:43:04.810
So we have tracing.

00:43:04.810 --> 00:43:06.765
Of course, logging
is centralized.

00:43:06.765 --> 00:43:08.530
If we jump back to
the slides, I'll

00:43:08.530 --> 00:43:12.580
cover a couple of other quick,
nice features of the Cloud

00:43:12.580 --> 00:43:16.180
Platform The other one
is the Live Debugger.

00:43:16.180 --> 00:43:20.590
So if you want to do real-time
introspection of application

00:43:20.590 --> 00:43:23.650
states, the Stackdriver Debugger
now allows you to do that.

00:43:23.650 --> 00:43:27.350
It works really well with Go,
and it works with Kubernetes.

00:43:27.350 --> 00:43:30.784
The other thing is
Google Cloud Endpoints.

00:43:30.784 --> 00:43:32.450
If you haven't heard
of Cloud Endpoints,

00:43:32.450 --> 00:43:34.549
and you're interested
in this, Dan Ciruli

00:43:34.549 --> 00:43:36.340
is giving a talk on
this, right afterwards.

00:43:36.340 --> 00:43:38.380
What Cloud Endpoints
allows you to do

00:43:38.380 --> 00:43:43.360
is to provide some additional
robustness and automation--

00:43:43.360 --> 00:43:47.980
and centralization
around your APIs.

00:43:47.980 --> 00:43:49.630
Happens to work
really well with gRPC.

00:43:49.630 --> 00:43:52.210
And this gives you features
like a dashboard for detailed

00:43:52.210 --> 00:43:56.140
logging of RPC traffic,
for detailed monitoring

00:43:56.140 --> 00:43:59.410
of your RPCs, and latencies--
again, across the system,

00:43:59.410 --> 00:44:00.780
if necessary--

00:44:00.780 --> 00:44:03.020
and things like
centralized access control.

00:44:03.020 --> 00:44:04.030
So if you ever get
to a point, where

00:44:04.030 --> 00:44:06.113
you're thinking about
publishing one of these APIs

00:44:06.113 --> 00:44:08.770
to make them consumable to
the rest of the world, all

00:44:08.770 --> 00:44:10.895
of those things that you
might need to think about,

00:44:10.895 --> 00:44:14.590
like user-based quartering
and ACL management.

00:44:14.590 --> 00:44:18.014
Cloud Endpoints
grossly simplifies

00:44:18.014 --> 00:44:19.180
the process of building app.

00:44:19.180 --> 00:44:22.360
And again, works
really well with gRPC.

00:44:22.360 --> 00:44:25.710
[MUSIC PLAYING]

